[36m(TaskRunner pid=1898318)[0m assistant
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1898318)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1898318)[0m [response] To find the probability that the game will be won before the fourth turn, we need to consider the probability of winning on the first, second, or third turn.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1898318)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1898318)[0m The probability of winning on a single turn is the product of the probability of rolling a 1 or a 6 on the die and the probability of getting heads on the coin. This is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1898318)[0m \[ P(\text{win on a single turn}) = \frac{2}{6} \times \frac{1}{2} = \frac{1}{6} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1898318)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1898318)[0m The probability of not winning on a single turn is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1898318)[0m \[ P(\text{not win on a single turn}) = 1 - \frac{1}{6} = \frac{5}{6} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1898318)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1898318)[0m The probability of winning before the fourth turn is the sum of the probabilities of winning on the first, second, or third turn. This can be calculated as:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1898318)[0m \[ P(\text{win before the fourth turn}) = P(\text{win on the first turn}) + P(\text{not win on the first turn}) \times P(\text{win on the second turn}) + P(\text{not win on the first two turns}) \times P(\text{win on the third turn}) \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1898318)[0m \[ = \frac{1}{6} + \frac{5}{6} \times \frac{1}{6} + \left(\frac{5}{6}\right)^2 \times \frac{1}{6} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1898318)[0m \[ = \frac{1}{6} + \frac{5}{36} + \frac{25}{216} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1898318)[0m \[ = \frac{36}{216} + \frac{30}{216} + \frac{25}{216} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1898318)[0m \[ = \frac{91}{216} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1898318)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1898318)[0m Therefore, the probability that the game will be won before the fourth turn is \(\boxed{\frac{91}{216}}\).
